STATUS          : NO per-frame gain term is fitted or applied. This merge assumes
                  constant photometry across the stack. The number above is the size
                  of that assumption, not a correction for it. Do NOT read gain from
                  EXIF to fix it: M24 measured two stacks drifting 6.84% and 8.93%
                  with CONSTANT EXIF, and coffee-bean moving only 16% of what its
                  EXIF change predicts (1.0443 measured vs 1.2800). Fit it.

--- ACCEPTANCE INVARIANT 1 of 2: flat-ROI noise (MEASURED) ---
roi             : x 1356 y 32 w 512 h 512 in plane coordinates
single frame 11 : sigma   76.272 DN   mean    413.50 DN   (no stacking at all)
model predicts  : sigma    8.770 DN for one green-luma sample at that level
  gate      sigma DN       mean DN      RATIO   winners in roi
   2.00      76.052        413.65      0.997x   18 distinct, median 9
                  PASS is <= 1.000x against the SAME-OPERATOR baseline: regularised argmax
                  measured 1.0739x on these frames and this ROI (fact 343). Fact 19's 1.253x
                  was a DIFFERENT focus operator and does not reproduce here -- it is not
                  this pipeline's baseline and must not be quoted as one. A plain unweighted
                  mean measured 0.534x, which is the floor, not a target, because of invariant 2.
                  CAVEAT (facts 469/441/442): this RATIO is a ratio of two RAW SPATIAL sds.
                  It is structure-dominated (these reference frames are 50.9-90.0% scene by
                  variance), it moves over a 1.60x range with the free choice of denominator
                  frame, and on a planted noise explosion it under-reported by 6.3x. Read it
                  as a smoke test, not as a noise measurement, until it is replaced by a
                  high-pass residual sd.

--- ACCEPTANCE INVARIANT 2 of 2: detail retention (MEASURED) ---
roi             : x 2215 y 699 w 512 h 512 in plane coordinates
                  THIS IS A ONE-SIDED TEST. It is valid against COLLAPSE and against
                  nothing else. mean|grad| can be RAISED by ringing, by amplified noise
                  and by manufactured edges, so an INCREASE is never evidence of quality.
                  MEASURED in the orchestrator's calibrated 1-D optics simulation (25
                  frames, oracle depth, latent mean|grad| 0.0392): at sigma 0.010 a Wiener
                  merge reads 0.0367 against selection's 0.0296 -- 24% MORE gradient --
                  while its gradient CORRELATION with truth is 0.7538 against 0.8595, i.e.
                  12% LESS faithful. At sigma 0.050 argmax reads 0.0455, ABOVE the ground
                  truth. A mean-gradient test scores both of those as wins. It must not.
                  MEAN |GRAD| IS ALSO CONFOUNDED BY NOISE and that confound has the same
                  sign as the effect: cutting sigma cuts gradient with zero blur, so
                  an uncorrected reading charges the merge for doing its job. `corrected`
                  is sqrt(raw^2 - model^2), the only column judged. `m2` is fact 24's
                  convention (M38 §1), reported so that comparison is legitimate.
single frame 11 : corrected  77.8539   raw rms  81.4488   model noise  23.9308   m2  29.5781   iso  48.9692
  gate     corrected     RETENTION   VERDICT      raw rms   model noise         m2        iso
   2.00     83.7881       1.076x   ok             86.0591       19.6400    29.3680    48.9877
                  The reference is ONE frame of the bracket, so much of this ROI is out
                  of focus in it and retention ABOVE 1.0 is expected -- that is the stack
                  finding focus the reference does not have. The failure this catches is
                  CORRECTED retention falling as the gate opens, which is fact 24's
                  collapse arriving and cannot be explained away as noise removal.
                  A rise is INVESTIGATE, not PASS: see the speckle companion below, which
                  is two-sided and shares a cause with manufactured detail.

--- COMPANION (two-sided): winner-index speckle (MEASURED) ---
index speckle   : 0.022% of pixels differ by more than 2 from their 3x3 median index
                  On the REGULARISED winner, the same statistic and tolerance `strehl stack`
                  reports for argmax. Unlike mean|grad| this is two-sided: a real scene gives
                  smooth depth with sharp edges, and BOTH over-smoothing and manufactured
                  detail move it away from that. Fact 23's reference points are 34.4% (ring
                  operator, unregularised) and 0.868% (Tenengrad, regularised).

